{"id":"gpt2-token-and-position-embeddings","t":"GPT-2 embeddings with a KV-cache offset and tied logits","tags":["Language Models","Embeddings","Transformers"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"gpt2_embed_and_logits","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"GPT-2 adds a learned position table to token embeddings and reuses the token table as the output projection.\n\nWrite `gpt2_embed_and_logits(ids, wte, wpe, h_final, past_len)`:\n\n- `wte` is `(V, d)`, `wpe` is `(n_ctx, d)`. `ids` are the **new** tokens; `past_len` tokens are already in the KV cache, so the new tokens sit at positions `past_len, past_len + 1, ...`.\n- Raise `ValueError` if `past_len + len(ids) > n_ctx`.\n- Input embeddings: `x = wte[ids] + wpe[positions]`.\n- Logits: `h_final @ wte.T`, where `h_final` is the final hidden state for the new tokens (after the final LayerNorm, given to you).\n\nReturn `(x, logits)`.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef gpt2_embed_and_logits(ids, wte, wpe, h_final, past_len):\n    \"\"\"Return (x, logits).\"\"\"\n    wte, wpe = np.asarray(wte), np.asarray(wpe)\n    x = wte[ids] + wpe[: len(ids)]\n    return x, np.asarray(h_final) @ wte.T\n","hints":["During cached decoding the new token is not at position 0; offset the position slice by past_len.","Weight tying means there is no separate output matrix: the logits are dot products with every token embedding."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef gpt2_embed_and_logits(ids, wte, wpe, h_final, past_len):\n    wte, wpe = np.asarray(wte, float), np.asarray(wpe, float)\n    T = len(ids)\n    if past_len + T > len(wpe):\n        raise ValueError(\"sequence exceeds the context window\")\n    x = wte[np.asarray(ids, int)] + wpe[past_len:past_len + T]\n    return x, np.asarray(h_final, float) @ wte.T\n","ex_md":"## Learned positions\nGPT-2 learns one vector per absolute position up to 1024. This is simple and works, but the model cannot run past `n_ctx`, which is why later models moved to rotary or relative schemes that extrapolate further.\n\n## The cache offset bug\nWith a KV cache each decoding step feeds one token. If you add `wpe[0]` instead of `wpe[past_len]`, every generated token believes it is at the start of the sequence, and output quality collapses after a few tokens even though nothing crashes.\n\n## Weight tying\nSharing `wte` between input and output saves $Vd$ parameters (38M for GPT-2 small) and couples the two spaces: tokens with similar embeddings get similar logits. Most decoder models of that era tied weights; some larger later models untie them.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- What happens to the tied logits if you scale `wte` during training?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"[4, 5, 7]\narray([[ 0.33043708, -1.30315723,  0.90535587,  0.44637457],\n       [-0.53695324,  0.5811181 ,  0.3645724 ,  0.2941325 ],\n       [ 0.02842224,  0.54671299, -0.73645409, -0.16290995],\n       [-0.48211931,  0.59884621,  0.03972211, -0.29245675],\n       [-0.78190846, -0.25719224,  0.00814218, -0.27560291],\n       [ 1.29406381,  1.00672432, -2.71116248, -1.88901325],\n       [-0.17477209, -0.42219041,  0.213643  ,  0.21732193],\n       [ 2.11783876, -1.11202076, -0.37760501,  2.04277161],\n       [ 0.646703  ,  0.66306337, -0.51400637, -1.64807517],\n       [ 0.16746474,  0.10901409, -1.22735205, -0.6 …\narray([[-0.07204368, -0.94475162, -0.09826997,  0.09548303],\n       [ 0.03558624, -0.50629166,  0.59374807,  0.89116695],\n       [ 0.3208483 , -0.81823023,  0.73165228, -0.50144002],\n       [ 0.87916062, -1.07178742,  0.9144672 , -0.02006345],\n       [-1.24874889, -0.31389947,  0.05410228,  0.27279134],\n       [-0.98218812, -1.10737305,  0.19958453, -0.46674962],\n       [ 0.23550561,  0.75951952, -1.64878737,  0.25438812],\n       [ 1.22464697, -0.29752684, -0.81081458,  0.75224383]])\narray([[ 0.25344652,  0.89588307, -0.34521571, -1.48181827],\n       [-0.11001076, -0.44582815,  0.77532382,  0.19363285],\n       [-1.63084923, -1.19516308,  0.88378904,  0.67976502]])\n0","out":"(array([[-0.85395214,